# tmenu TODO

This document tracks issues that must be addressed to make tmenu actually usable
and maintainable in 2024 and beyond.

---

## 1. CI/CD: Replace Travis CI

Travis CI shut down its free tier for open-source projects. The `.travis.yml`
file and the build badge in README.md are dead.

- Migrate to GitHub Actions (add `.github/workflows/build.yml`)
- Test on Linux and macOS matrix
- Remove `.travis.yml`
- Fix the broken badge in README.md

---

## 2. Correctness: UTF-8 Detection Threshold Is Wrong

In two places the code uses `> 0x70` to detect multi-byte UTF-8 sequences:

- `menu.c:248`  (`displayprompt`)
- `textbuffer.c:25` (`bufbackward`)

The correct threshold for a UTF-8 lead byte is `>= 0xC0`; continuation bytes
have the pattern `10xxxxxx` (i.e. `(byte & 0xC0) == 0x80`).  The current
threshold `> 0x70` incorrectly treats printable ASCII characters `0x71–0x7F`
(`q r s t u v w x y z { | } ~ DEL`) as multi-byte lead bytes, causing cursor
movement and display to break for any input containing those characters.

---

## 3. Safety: Signal Handlers Call Non-Async-Signal-Safe Functions

`update_current_menu_size` (called on `SIGWINCH`) invokes `Menu.display`, which
calls `fprintf` and other stdio functions.  `handle_interrupt` (called on
`SIGINT`) calls `Terminal.destroy` (which uses `tcsetattr`, `fclose`, `free`)
and then `exit`.  None of these are async-signal-safe per POSIX.

Fix: in each signal handler set a `volatile sig_atomic_t` flag only; act on
the flag in the main event loop.

---

## 4. Safety: `SIGTERM` Not Handled — Terminal Left in Raw Mode

If the process receives `SIGTERM` (e.g. `kill`, system shutdown) the terminal
is left in raw/no-echo mode, breaking the calling shell.  Add a `SIGTERM`
handler that restores terminal settings before exiting.

---

## 5. Usability: Cancellation Exits With Code 0

Pressing `Ctrl-C` restores the terminal and exits with code `0`, making it
impossible for scripts to distinguish "user confirmed a selection" from "user
cancelled".  The conventional behaviour is to exit with a non-zero code (e.g.
`1` or `130`) on cancellation.  Additionally, the `Escape` key is not bound at
all; it should also cancel with a non-zero exit code.

---

## 6. Usability: Arrow Keys Not Supported

Only Emacs-style bindings work.  Most users expect `↑`/`↓` to navigate the
list and `←`/`→` to move the cursor.  Arrow keys send multi-byte escape
sequences (`\033[A`, `\033[B`, etc.); the current single-character keymap
array (`KEYMAP[256]`) cannot represent them.

The input loop in `main.c` reads up to 8 bytes at a time but only dispatches
on `buf[0]`, silently discarding the rest of any escape sequence.

---

## 7. Correctness: `bufexpand` Has an Off-by-One Error

In `buffer.c:88`, after relocating the after-gap content into the new
allocation, `gapend` is updated as:

```c
self->gapend = new_capacity - self->gapend;
```

`bufafter()` returns `data + gapend - 1`.  With the new value, that pointer
lands one byte before the relocated content.  The correct update is:

```c
self->gapend = new_capacity - (old_capacity - self->gapend);
```

or equivalently `self->gapend += BUFFER_GROW_BY`.

---

## 8. Robustness: Hard Item-Length Limit Silently Truncates Input

`getitem()` in `main.c` reads at most `MENU_ITEM_MAX_SIZE - 1` (2047) bytes per
line and discards the rest without any warning.  Items longer than this are
silently truncated, which can cause incorrect matching or misleading output.
Either increase the limit, make it dynamic, or emit a warning on truncation.

---

## 9. Robustness: Hard Item-Count Limit Is Easy to Hit

`MENU_MAX_ITEMS` is 10 000.  Commands like `git ls-files` on large monorepos
easily exceed this.  When the limit is hit the program prints an error to
stderr but continues—silently dropping items and giving incomplete results with
no non-zero exit code.

Options: raise the limit, make capacity fully dynamic (no hard cap), or exit
with an error.

---

## 10. Code Quality: Magic Numbers for Line Navigation

`action_beginning_of_line` and `action_end_of_line` in `main.c` move the
cursor by the hardcoded value `10000`:

```c
TextBuffer.backward(Menu.buffer(menu), 10000);
TextBuffer.forward(Menu.buffer(menu), 10000);
```

This works incidentally because no line is expected to exceed 10 000 chars, but
it is fragile and misleading.  Add `TextBuffer.beginning_of_line()` /
`TextBuffer.end_of_line()` operations, or use `SIZE_MAX` with a comment.

---

## 11. Code Quality: Redundant Match Logic in `menumatch`

`menumatch()` first tests `strstr(item, pattern)` and, only if that fails,
falls back to `fnmatch("*pattern*", item, 0)`.  A `fnmatch` glob `*pattern*`
matches every string that `strstr` matches (and more), so the `strstr` branch
is effectively a subset of what fnmatch would do anyway.  The stated feature
is "fuzzy matching" but the implementation is just substring search with an
unreachable glob fallback.  Decide on a matching strategy and implement it
cleanly.

---

## 12. Code Quality: `menu_test.c` Includes `menu.c` Directly

```c
#include "menu.c"
```

This is a common workaround to access internal (`static`) functions from tests,
but it means the translation unit is compiled twice and changes to the
compilation flags of the library unit may not apply to the test.  Either expose
internal helpers through a test-only header, or use link-time symbol visibility.

---

## 13. Code Quality: `script/test` Uses Uninitialized Variable

In `script/test`, `TEST_VALGRIND` is only set if `valgrind` is found.  The
final `exit $(( TEST_DIFFERENCES + TEST_VALGRIND ))` will expand to an
arithmetic expression with an unset variable, which evaluates to `0` in most
shells—masking a situation where only the Valgrind check would have caught an
error.  Initialise `TEST_VALGRIND=0` at the top of the script.

---

## 14. Build System: Fragmented Compile Scripts

The build uses `script/compile` (which detects the OS and dispatches to
`script/compile-darwin` or a fallback), rather than using standard build
system conventions (`CFLAGS`, `CC`).  This makes it hard to cross-compile, use
alternative compilers, or integrate with package managers.  Consider replacing
the script dispatch with standard Makefile variables and a single rule that
honours `CC` and `CFLAGS`.

---

## 15. Build System: Incorrect Dependency for `textbuffer.o`

In the Makefile:

```make
textbuffer.o: textbuffer.h textbuffer.c buffer.h buffer.c
```

`textbuffer.o` should not depend on `buffer.c` (a source file); it should
depend on `buffer.h` (the header) and on the `buffer.o` object file if ordering
matters.  Depending on a `.c` source file in a `.o` rule is unusual and can
cause spurious recompilation.

---

## 16. Packaging: No Version Number, No Release Artefacts

`config.h` defines `PROGRAM_NAME` but no `PROGRAM_VERSION`.  There is no
`--version` flag, no version tag in the man page date, and no `dist` Makefile
target.  Add a version string and wire it to `--version` output and the man
page.
